{"stream": "process", "run_id": "res-6f2cda551cbf", "event": "mcp_tool_result", "ts": 1787836628.3255134, "tool": "get_blog_summary", "attempt": 1, "kind": "object", "keys": ["found", "summary", "ticket_hs_id", "warnings"], "result_preview": "{'found': True, 'summary': {'subject': 'Insurance AI Claims and Underwriting Governance', 'blog_summary': \"AI in Claims and Underwriting: The Governance to Show\\n\\nThis document argues that in insurance, AI-generated outputs in claims and underwriting are effectively regulated decisions, so the control layer (citations, human review, logging) must be a core part of the product rather than an afterthought. It describes regulatory expectations, including the NAIC's model bulletin, FCRA adverse action rules, and state unfair claims settlement practices laws, which require carriers to justify denials with specific policy language. It outlines what market conduct examiners look for and recommends building an 'exam-ready' evidence pack in advance, including an AI inventory, written governance program, decision logs, testing evidence, and complaint linkage. The document also stresses that models, prompts, and retrieval corpora change over time, so carriers need version pinning, frozen evaluation sets, override tracking, and formal change approval to maintain defensibility. It closes by referencing AI Definitive's 'Governed-by-Design' approach and a free Claude Readiness Assessment.\\n\\nKey points:\\n- AI drafts in claims/underwriting can become regulated decisions instantly, so governance must be built into the product itself.\\n- Retrieval must cite the exact policy form, edition, and endorsement in force for the specific policy and date of loss.\\n- Denials require a second human gate: a licensed adjuster or underwriter reviews and approves before the decision is finalized.\\n- Regulatory frameworks include the NAIC model bulletin, FCRA adverse action rules, and state unfair claims settlement practices laws, with expectations varying by state.\\n- Carriers with multi-state footprints should build to the strictest state standard rather than running multiple explainability standards.\\n- Market conduct examiners expect an AI inventory, written governance program, decision logs, testing evidence, and complaint traceability, ideally prepared in advance.\\n- Model, prompt, and retrieval corpus changes require version pinning, frozen evaluation sets re-run before promotion, override tracking, and formal change approval.\\n- A rising adjuster override rate is an early signal of model drift, often before other metrics show it.\\n\\nTechnologies: Claude, NAIC model bulletin on AI use, FCRA (Fair Credit Reporting Act)\", 'blog_published_at': '2026-08-24T15:00:00Z', 'blog_industry': 'FINANCIAL_SERVICES'}, 'ticket_hs_id': '330600234700', 'warnings': []}"}
{"stream": "process", "run_id": "res-6f2cda551cbf", "event": "step_out", "ts": 1787836628.3269842, "step": "read_blog", "status": "ok", "duration_ms": 771.4, "objectId": "330600234700", "blog_industry": "FINANCIAL_SERVICES", "summary_chars": 2314, "ticket_id": "330600234700", "summary_preview": "AI in Claims and Underwriting: The Governance to Show\n\nThis document argues that in insurance, AI-generated outputs in claims and underwriting are effectively regulated decisions, so the control layer (citations, human review, logging) must be a core part of the product rather than an afterthought. It describes regulatory expectations, including the NAIC's model bulletin, FCRA adverse action rules, and state unfair claims settlement practices laws, which require carriers to justify denials with specific policy language. It outlines what market conduct examiners look for and recommends building an 'exam-ready' evidence pack in advance, including an AI inventory, written governance program, decision logs, testing evidence, and complaint linkage. The document also stresses that models, prompts, and retrieval corpora change over time, so carriers need version pinning, frozen evaluation sets, override tracking, and formal change approval to maintain defensibility. It closes by referencing AI Definitive's 'Governed-by-Design' approach and a free Claude Readiness Assessment.\n\nKey points:\n- AI drafts in claims/underwriting can become regulated decisions instantly, so governance must be built into the product itself.\n- Retrieval must cite the exact policy form, edition, and endorsement in force for the specific policy and date of loss.\n- Denials require a second human gate: a licensed adjuster or underwriter reviews and approves before the decision is finalized.\n- Regulatory frameworks include the NAIC model bulletin, FCRA adverse action rules, and state unfair claims settlement practices laws, with expectations varying by state.\n- Carriers with multi-state footprints should build to the strictest state standard rather than running multiple explainability standards.\n- Market conduct examiners expect an AI inventory, written governance program, decision logs, testing evidence, and complaint traceability, ideally prepared in advance.\n- Model, prompt, and retrieval corpus changes require version pinning, frozen evaluation sets re-run before promotion, override tracking, and formal change approval.\n- A rising adjuster override rate is an early signal of model drift, often before other metrics show it.\n\nTechnologies: Claude, NAIC model bulletin on AI use, FCRA (Fair Credit Reporting Act)"}
