{"stream": "process", "run_id": "res-6f2cda551cbf", "event": "model_request", "ts": 1787836643.830588, "model": "claude-sonnet-4-6", "max_tokens": 2000, "search_enabled": true, "search_tool": "web_search_20250305", "search_max_uses": 5, "timeout_s": 90, "prompt_chars": 2948, "system_chars": 1496, "endpoint": "anthropic.messages.create", "sent_keys": ["max_tokens", "messages", "model", "system", "tools"], "model_configured": "", "allowed_domains": [], "blocked_domains": [], "system_preview": "# Research prompt — lead_context\n\nYou are the Research Agent for a B2B lead-qualification system. You produce ONE\nshort note that will be stored on a HubSpot contact as `lead_context` and read\nlater by an email agent writing first-touch outreach.\n\n## What you are given\n\n- **The lead**: name, job title, the company they work for, and its industry.\n- **The published post**: a summary of an article our side just published.\n\n## What to do\n\n1. Search the web for what is currently true about **this company** and **this\n   industry**, focused on the themes in the published post.\n2. Write a note that connects the post's themes to that specific company's\n   situation — what they actually do, how the theme lands for them, and the one\n   question a salesperson could open with.\n\n## Rules\n\n- **Ground every claim.** If the search did not support it, do not write it.\n  No invented funding rounds, headcounts, customers, or product names.\n- **Be specific to this company.** A note that would read identically for any\n  company in the industry is a failed note.\n- **Never address the lead directly** and never write email copy. This is\n  context *for* the writer, not the message itself.\n- Target ~160 words, one paragraph, plain prose — no headings, no\n  bullet lists, no markdown.\n- End with one open question a rep could ask, phrased for a first conversation.\n- If the search finds little about the company, say so plainly and stay at the\n  industry level rather than padding with generic claims.\n", "prompt_preview": "Research this lead and write the lead_context note.\n\n## The lead\n- Name: Harish Bandla\n- Job title: President\n- Company: Brex\n- Industry: FINANCIAL_SERVICES\n- Company website: https://www.brex.com\n- About the company: Financial services company providing corporate cards, expense management, and integrated finance software to startups, scaled businesses, and ecommerce brands.\n\n## The post we published\n- Industry: FINANCIAL_SERVICES\n- Published: 2026-08-24T15:00:00Z\n- Summary:\nAI in Claims and Underwriting: The Governance to Show\n\nThis document argues that in insurance, AI-generated outputs in claims and underwriting are effectively regulated decisions, so the control layer (citations, human review, logging) must be a core part of the product rather than an afterthought. It describes regulatory expectations, including the NAIC's model bulletin, FCRA adverse action rules, and state unfair claims settlement practices laws, which require carriers to justify denials with specific policy language. It outlines what market conduct examiners look for and recommends building an 'exam-ready' evidence pack in advance, including an AI inventory, written governance program, decision logs, testing evidence, and complaint linkage. The document also stresses that models, prompts, and retrieval corpora change over time, so carriers need version pinning, frozen evaluation sets, override tracking, and formal change approval to maintain defensibility. It closes by referencing AI Definitive's 'Governed-by-Design' approach and a free Claude Readiness Assessment.\n\nKey points:\n- AI drafts in claims/underwriting can become regulated decisions instantly, so governance must be built into the product itself.\n- Retrieval must cite the exact policy form, edition, and endorsement in force for the specific policy and date of loss.\n- Denials require a second human gate: a licensed adjuster or underwriter reviews and approves before the decision is finalized.\n- Regulatory frameworks include the NAIC model bulletin, FCRA adverse action rules, and state unfair claims settlement practices laws, with expectations varying by state.\n- Carriers with multi-state footprints should build to the strictest state standard rather than running multiple explainability standards.\n- Market conduct examiners expect an AI inventory, written governance program, decision logs, testing evidence, and complaint traceability, ideally prepared in advance.\n- Model, prompt, and retrieval corpus changes require version pinning, frozen evaluation sets re-run before promotion, override tracking, and formal change approval.\n- A rising adjuster override rate is an early signal of model drift, often before other metrics show it.\n\nTechnologies: Claude, NAIC model bulletin on AI use, FCRA (Fair Credit Reporting Act)\n\n## Your task\nSearch the web for what is true right now about Brex and the FINANCIAL_SERVICES industry, focused on the themes above. Then write the note.", "payload": {"model": "claude-sonnet-4-6", "max_tokens": 2000, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Research this lead and write the lead_context note.\n\n## The lead\n- Name: Harish Bandla\n- Job title: President\n- Company: Brex\n- Industry: FINANCIAL_SERVICES\n- Company website: https://www.brex.com\n- About the company: Financial services company providing corporate cards, expense management, and integrated finance software to startups, scaled businesses, and ecommerce brands.\n\n## The post we published\n- Industry: FINANCIAL_SERVICES\n- Published: 2026-08-24T15:00:00Z\n- Summary:\nAI in Claims and Underwriting: The Governance to Show\n\nThis document argues that in insurance, AI-generated outputs in claims and underwriting are effectively regulated decisions, so the control layer (citations, human review, logging) must be a core part of the product rather than an afterthought. It describes regulatory expectations, including the NAIC's model bulletin, FCRA adverse action rules, and state unfair claims settlement practices laws, which require carriers to justify denials with specific policy language. It outlines what market conduct examiners look for and recommends building an 'exam-ready' evidence pack in advance, including an AI inventory, written governance program, decision logs, testing evidence, and complaint linkage. The document also stresses that models, prompts, and retrieval corpora change over time, so carriers need version pinning, frozen evaluation sets, override tracking, and formal change approval to maintain defensibility. It closes by referencing AI Definitive's 'Governed-by-Design' approach and a free Claude Readiness Assessment.\n\nKey points:\n- AI drafts in claims/underwriting can become regulated decisions instantly, so governance must be built into the product itself.\n- Retrieval must cite the exact policy form, edition, and endorsement in force for the specific policy and date of loss.\n- Denials require a second human gate: a licensed adjuster or underwriter reviews and approves before the decision is finalized.\n- Regulatory frameworks include the NAIC model bulletin, FCRA adverse action rules, and state unfair claims settlement practices laws, with expectations varying by state.\n- Carriers with multi-state footprints should build to the strictest state standard rather than running multiple explainability standards.\n- Market conduct examiners expect an AI inventory, written governance program, decision logs, testing evidence, and complaint traceability, ideally prepared in advance.\n- Model, prompt, and retrieval corpus changes require version pinning, frozen evaluation sets re-run before promotion, override tracking, and formal change approval.\n- A rising adjuster override rate is an early signal of model drift, often before other metrics show it.\n\nTechnologies: Claude, NAIC model bulletin on AI use, FCRA (Fair Credit Reporting Act)\n\n## Your task\nSearch the web for what is true right now about Brex and the FINANCIAL_SERVICES industry, focused on the themes above. Then write the note."}], "tools": [{"type": "web_search_20250305", "name": "web_search", "max_uses": 5}], "system": "# Research prompt — lead_context\n\nYou are the Research Agent for a B2B lead-qualification system. You produce ONE\nshort note that will be stored on a HubSpot contact as `lead_context` and read\nlater by an email agent writing first-touch outreach.\n\n## What you are given\n\n- **The lead**: name, job title, the company they work for, and its industry.\n- **The published post**: a summary of an article our side just published.\n\n## What to do\n\n1. Search the web for what is currently true about **this company** and **this\n   industry**, focused on the themes in the published post.\n2. Write a note that connects the post's themes to that specific company's\n   situation — what they actually do, how the theme lands for them, and the one\n   question a salesperson could open with.\n\n## Rules\n\n- **Ground every claim.** If the search did not support it, do not write it.\n  No invented funding rounds, headcounts, customers, or product names.\n- **Be specific to this company.** A note that would read identically for any\n  company in the industry is a failed note.\n- **Never address the lead directly** and never write email copy. This is\n  context *for* the writer, not the message itself.\n- Target ~160 words, one paragraph, plain prose — no headings, no\n  bullet lists, no markdown.\n- End with one open question a rep could ask, phrased for a first conversation.\n- If the search finds little about the company, say so plainly and stay at the\n  industry level rather than padding with generic claims.\n"}}
{"stream": "process", "run_id": "res-6f2cda551cbf", "event": "model_response", "ts": 1787836661.9021683, "model": "claude-sonnet-4-6", "duration_ms": 18068.4, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "input_tokens": 23057, "output_tokens": 607, "web_search_requests": 3, "searches": 3, "sources": 25, "source_urls": ["https://www.zenml.io/llmops-database/ai-powered-financial-assistant-for-automated-expense-management", "https://ramp.com/blog/brex-vs-bill", "https://investor.capitalone.com/news-releases/news-release-details/capital-one-acquire-brex", "https://www.brex.com/", "https://www.capitalone.com/about/newsroom/capital-one-completes-acquisition-of-brex/", "https://www.brex.com/product/spend-management", "https://www.brex.com/journal/accelerate-expense-management-with-ai", "https://research.com/software/reviews/brex", "https://workflowautomation.net/reviews/brex", "https://www.brex.com/journal/press/brex-helps-power-open-ai-global-spend-and-financial-operations", "https://www.gartner.com/reviews/product/brex", "https://saifr.ai/blog/2026-trends-ai-and-compliance-in-financial-services", "https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/brex-helps-power-openai-global-spend-and-financial-operations-302717397.html", "https://www.brex.com/journal/from-chaos-to-control-with-ai", "https://www.aicerts.ai/news/financial-ops-agents-brexs-engine-for-agentic-commerce/", "https://aurascape.ai/answers/ai-compliance-frameworks-standards-and-governance-for-banks-investment-firms/", "https://insightdistillery.com/articles/2026-01-31-financial-software-learns-to-think/", "https://www.wolterskluwer.com/en/expert-insights/the-fintech-landscape-in-2026", "https://www.innreg.com/blog/fintech-trends-2026", "https://hesfintech.com/blog/all-legislative-trends-regulating-ai-in-lending/", "https://neontri.com/blog/ai-credit-scoring/", "https://www.techfunnel.com/fintech/fintech-compliance-ai-cfos-2026/", "https://practiceguides.chambers.com/practice-guides/fintech-2026/usa", "https://fintechbloom.com/ai-compliance-in-fintech-who-wins-and-loses-in-2026/", "https://www.businesswire.com/news/home/20221018005116/en/Provenir-Named-Finalist-for-Best-Credit-Risk-Solution-and-Best-Compliance-Regulatory-Technology-Solution-in-the-Credit-Collections-Technology-Awards"], "chars": 1671, "text_preview": "Here is the `lead_context` note for Harish Bandla at Brex:\n\n---\n\nBrex completed its acquisition by Capital One in April 2026 and describes itself as an AI-native platform for corporate cards, expense management, and real-time payments — one that also leverages AI agents to help customers automate complex workflows and reduce manual review. That last detail is the crux of the governance conversation. Brex's credit underwriting model evaluates a company's cash balance, funding stage, and spending patterns rather than personal credit scores — meaning AI-generated signals are at the heart of consequential financial decisions, not just back-office automation. Regulators have made clear that banking and consumer-protection rules apply regardless of whether a decision is made by a human or an AI, and for credit products lenders must provide specific, accurate adverse action reasons even when using complex models. ECOA and Regulation B impose that duty on every adverse credit action, and model opacity is not a defense. Now folded into Capital One — a regulated bank with deep examiner relationships — Brex already practices AI reliability engineering with evaluation frameworks and multi-agent orchestration, but the post's argument about version pinning, decision logs, and exam-ready governance packs applies directly to any AI outputs that touch credit limits or spend approvals. The one question worth opening with: as Brex's AI agents take on more autonomous roles in credit decisioning and spend controls under Capital One's regulatory umbrella, what does the current audit trail look like if an examiner asks why a specific credit limit was set or changed?"}
