ue right now about MoneyLion and the FINANCIAL_SERVICES industry, focused on the themes above. Then write the note."}], "tools": [{"type": "web_search_20250305", "name": "web_search", "max_uses": 5}], "system": "# Research prompt — lead_context\n\nYou are the Research Agent for a B2B lead-qualification system. You produce ONE\nshort note that will be stored on a HubSpot contact as `lead_context` and read\nlater by an email agent writing first-touch outreach.\n\n## What you are given\n\n- **The lead**: name, job title, the company they work for, and its industry.\n- **The published post**: a summary of an article our side just published.\n\n## What to do\n\n1. Search the web for what is currently true about **this company** and **this\n   industry**, focused on the themes in the published post.\n2. Write a note that connects the post's themes to that specific company's\n   situation — what they actually do, how the theme lands for them, and the one\n   question a salesperson could open with.\n\n## Rules\n\n- **Ground every claim.** If the search did not support it, do not write it.\n  No invented funding rounds, headcounts, customers, or product names.\n- **Be specific to this company.** A note that would read identically for any\n  company in the industry is a failed note.\n- **Never address the lead directly** and never write email copy. This is\n  context *for* the writer, not the message itself.\n- Target ~160 words, one paragraph, plain prose — no headings, no\n  bullet lists, no markdown.\n- End with one open question a rep could ask, phrased for a first conversation.\n- If the search finds little about the company, say so plainly and stay at the\n  industry level rather than padding with generic claims.\n"}}
{"stream": "process", "run_id": "res-6f2cda551cbf", "event": "model_response", "ts": 1787836718.9721096, "model": "claude-sonnet-4-6", "duration_ms": 22815.9, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "input_tokens": 44336, "output_tokens": 815, "web_search_requests": 4, "searches": 4, "sources": 30, "source_urls": ["https://www.v7labs.com/blog/ai-commercial-loan-underwriting", "https://oscilar.com/blog/ai-credit-underwriting", "https://tearsheet.co/artificial-intelligence/meet-the-ai-lending-officer-how-algorithms-are-taking-over-credit-decisions/", "https://delphos.co/news/blog/ai-underwriting-private-credit-in-emerging-markets-the-2026-inflection/", "https://timvero.com/blog/how-ai-and-automation-are-transforming-lending", "https://bettermarkets.substack.com/p/who-gets-the-money-how-ai-will-impact", "https://www.ccgcatalyst.com/thought-leadership/commentary/lending-transformation-ai-private-credit-and-the-battle-for-borrowers/", "https://www.plantemoran.com/explore-our-thinking/insight/2025/03/q1-2025-compliance-updates-for-financial-institutions", "https://www.consumerfinance.gov/compliance/compliance-resources/other-applicable-requirements/fair-credit-reporting-act/", "https://www.mba.org/docs/default-source/conferences/2025/crmc25/crm25-fcra.pdf?sfvrsn=9db62adf_1", "https://thekimlawfirmllc.com/moneylion-credit-report/", "https://www.innreg.com/blog/lending-regulation-guide", "https://www.aba.com/banking-topics/compliance/acts/fair-credit-reporting-act", "https://nationalmortgageprofessional.com/news/cfpb-issues-ai-underwriting-guidance-adverse-action-notices", "https://www.lexology.com/library/detail.aspx?g=536c2179-f411-4793-ae9c-1bc3c44e3651", "https://www.venable.com/insights/publications/2026/02/ai-in-financial-services-popular-use-cases", "https://hesfintech.com/blog/all-legislative-trends-regulating-ai-in-lending/", "https://www.loanpro.io/blog/ai-in-lending-trends/", "https://www.theleveragedyears.com/ai-regulation-news/cfpb-ai-fair-lending-rollback-what-still-binds-lenders-2026", "https://crosscheckcompliance.com/resources/articles/automated-underwriting-ai-fair-lending-risk/", "https://compliance4creditunions.com/ai-in-underwriting-compliance-risk-lessons/", "https://www.creditandcollectionnews.com/cfpb-issues-ai-underwriting-guidance-on-adverse-action-notices/", "https://lenderbox.ai/blog-posts/ai-regulatory-guidance-commercial-underwriting", "https://www.nerdwallet.com/personal-loans/learn/moneylion-cash-advance", "https://money.usnews.com/loans/personal-loans/reviews/moneylion-personal", "https://lendedu.com/blog/moneylion-cash-advance-review/", "https://apps.apple.com/us/app/moneylion-banking-cash-back/id1064677082", "https://www.leadsightpro.com/mobile-app-development/the-future-of-cash-advance-apps-2026-top-apps-like-moneylion-ai-trends", "https://creditbooster.ai/learn/moneylion-alternatives/", "https://play.google.com/store/apps/details?id=com.moneylion&hl=en_US"], "chars": 2062, "text_preview": "Here is the `lead_context` note:\n\n---\n\nMoneyLion operates as a digital financial wellness platform that bundles cash advances, mobile banking, credit-building tools, investing, and personal finance management within a single app — serving users holistically rather than through any single lending product. Advance limits are determined by direct deposits, account transaction history, and other proprietary factors, and MoneyLion has partnered with Plaid to enhance credit decisioning using real-time cash flow data, with a stated goal of expanding access to credit for underserved borrowers. That decisioning infrastructure sits squarely in the regulatory crosshairs of our post's themes: the CFPB issued Circular 2026-03 in May 2026 advising that lenders using machine-learning underwriting models remain fully responsible under ECOA and Regulation B for providing specific, accurate reasons for adverse action — lenders cannot claim a black box \"told us to do it,\" and proprietary or uninterpretable models do not excuse compliance. Under FCRA, fintechs that pull or furnish consumer credit data must follow strict accuracy and disclosure standards, notify applicants of adverse actions, and maintain model transparency with clear documentation of credit decision factors. MoneyLion has already drawn regulatory scrutiny: in November 2025 the CFPB reached a $1.75 million settlement with MoneyLion after accusing the company of violating the Military Lending Act. Given this backdrop, fintech companies face model risk management challenges from ML models, and federal banking regulator guidance requires institutions to document model design and data sources, conduct independent validation, and perform ongoing performance monitoring. A strong opening question for the rep: *Given that MoneyLion's Instacash and Credit Builder limits are set by proprietary algorithms at scale, what does the current governance and audit-trail infrastructure look like if a regulator asks for a decision-by-decision log of why a specific borrower received a specific limit?*"}
