{"stream": "process", "run_id": "res-6f2cda551cbf", "event": "model_request", "ts": 1787836724.5617704, "model": "claude-sonnet-4-6", "max_tokens": 2000, "search_enabled": true, "search_tool": "web_search_20250305", "search_max_uses": 5, "timeout_s": 90, "prompt_chars": 2968, "system_chars": 1496, "endpoint": "anthropic.messages.create", "sent_keys": ["max_tokens", "messages", "model", "system", "tools"], "model_configured": "", "allowed_domains": [], "blocked_domains": [], "system_preview": "# Research prompt — lead_context\n\nYou are the Research Agent for a B2B lead-qualification system. You produce ONE\nshort note that will be stored on a HubSpot contact as `lead_context` and read\nlater by an email agent writing first-touch outreach.\n\n## What you are given\n\n- **The lead**: name, job title, the company they work for, and its industry.\n- **The published post**: a summary of an article our side just published.\n\n## What to do\n\n1. Search the web for what is currently true about **this company** and **this\n   industry**, focused on the themes in the published post.\n2. Write a note that connects the post's themes to that specific company's\n   situation — what they actually do, how the theme lands for them, and the one\n   question a salesperson could open with.\n\n## Rules\n\n- **Ground every claim.** If the search did not support it, do not write it.\n  No invented funding rounds, headcounts, customers, or product names.\n- **Be specific to this company.** A note that would read identically for any\n  company in the industry is a failed note.\n- **Never address the lead directly** and never write email copy. This is\n  context *for* the writer, not the message itself.\n- Target ~160 words, one paragraph, plain prose — no headings, no\n  bullet lists, no markdown.\n- End with one open question a rep could ask, phrased for a first conversation.\n- If the search finds little about the company, say so plainly and stay at the\n  industry level rather than padding with generic claims.\n", "prompt_preview": "Research this lead and write the lead_context note.\n\n## The lead\n- Name: Tejeshwar Thodeti\n- Job title: Head of Operations\n- Company: Stash\n- Industry: FINANCIAL_SERVICES\n- Company website: https://www.stash.com\n- About the company: Consumer fintech that lets everyday Americans start investing with as little as $5, pairing automated investment management with personalized advice and education.\n\n## The post we published\n- Industry: FINANCIAL_SERVICES\n- Published: 2026-08-24T15:00:00Z\n- Summary:\nAI in Claims and Underwriting: The Governance to Show\n\nThis document argues that in insurance, AI-generated outputs in claims and underwriting are effectively regulated decisions, so the control layer (citations, human review, logging) must be a core part of the product rather than an afterthought. It describes regulatory expectations, including the NAIC's model bulletin, FCRA adverse action rules, and state unfair claims settlement practices laws, which require carriers to justify denials with specific policy language. It outlines what market conduct examiners look for and recommends building an 'exam-ready' evidence pack in advance, including an AI inventory, written governance program, decision logs, testing evidence, and complaint linkage. The document also stresses that models, prompts, and retrieval corpora change over time, so carriers need version pinning, frozen evaluation sets, override tracking, and formal change approval to maintain defensibility. It closes by referencing AI Definitive's 'Governed-by-Design' approach and a free Claude Readiness Assessment.\n\nKey points:\n- AI drafts in claims/underwriting can become regulated decisions instantly, so governance must be built into the product itself.\n- Retrieval must cite the exact policy form, edition, and endorsement in force for the specific policy and date of loss.\n- Denials require a second human gate: a licensed adjuster or underwriter reviews and approves before the decision is finalized.\n- Regulatory frameworks include the NAIC model bulletin, FCRA adverse action rules, and state unfair claims settlement practices laws, with expectations varying by state.\n- Carriers with multi-state footprints should build to the strictest state standard rather than running multiple explainability standards.\n- Market conduct examiners expect an AI inventory, written governance program, decision logs, testing evidence, and complaint traceability, ideally prepared in advance.\n- Model, prompt, and retrieval corpus changes require version pinning, frozen evaluation sets re-run before promotion, override tracking, and formal change approval.\n- A rising adjuster override rate is an early signal of model drift, often before other metrics show it.\n\nTechnologies: Claude, NAIC model bulletin on AI use, FCRA (Fair Credit Reporting Act)\n\n## Your task\nSearch the web for what is true right now about Stash and the FINANCIAL_SERVICES industry, focused on the themes above. Then write the note.", "payload": {"model": "claude-sonnet-4-6", "max_tokens": 2000, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Research this lead and write the lead_context note.\n\n## The lead\n- Name: Tejeshwar Thodeti\n- Job title: Head of Operations\n- Company: Stash\n- Industry: FINANCIAL_SERVICES\n- Company website: https://www.stash.com\n- About the company: Consumer fintech that lets everyday Americans start investing with as little as $5, pairing automated investment management with personalized advice and education.\n\n## The post we published\n- Industry: FINANCIAL_SERVICES\n- Published: 2026-08-24T15:00:00Z\n- Summary:\nAI in Claims and Underwriting: The Governance to Show\n\nThis document argues that in insurance, AI-generated outputs in claims and underwriting are effectively regulated decisions, so the control layer (citations, human review, logging) must be a core part of the product rather than an afterthought. It describes regulatory expectations, including the NAIC's model bulletin, FCRA adverse action rules, and state unfair claims settlement practices laws, which require carriers to justify denials with specific policy language. It outlines what market conduct examiners look for and recommends building an 'exam-ready' evidence pack in advance, including an AI inventory, written governance program, decision logs, testing evidence, and complaint linkage. The document also stresses that models, prompts, and retrieval corpora change over time, so carriers need version pinning, frozen evaluation sets, override tracking, and formal change approval to maintain defensibility. It closes by referencing AI Definitive's 'Governed-by-Design' approach and a free Claude Readiness Assessment.\n\nKey points:\n- AI drafts in claims/underwriting can become regulated decisions instantly, so governance must be built into the product itself.\n- Retrieval must cite the exact policy form, edition, and endorsement in force for the specific policy and date of loss.\n- Denials require a second human gate: a licensed adjuster or underwriter reviews and approves before the decision is finalized.\n- Regulatory frameworks include the NAIC model bulletin, FCRA adverse action rules, and state unfair claims settlement practices laws, with expectations varying by state.\n- Carriers with multi-state footprints should build to the strictest state standard rather than running multiple explainability standards.\n- Market conduct examiners expect an AI inventory, written governance program, decision logs, testing evidence, and complaint traceability, ideally prepared in advance.\n- Model, prompt, and retrieval corpus changes require version pinning, frozen evaluation sets re-run before promotion, override tracking, and formal change approval.\n- A rising adjuster override rate is an early signal of model drift, often before other metrics show it.\n\nTechnologies: Claude, NAIC model bulletin on AI use, FCRA (Fair Credit Reporting Act)\n\n## Your task\nSearch the web for what is true right now about Stash and the FINANCIAL_SERVICES industry, focused on the themes above. Then write the note."}], "tools": [{"type": "web_search_20250305", "name": "web_search", "max_uses": 5}], "system": "# Research prompt — lead_context\n\nYou are the Research Agent for a B2B lead-qualification system. You produce ONE\nshort note that will be stored on a HubSpot contact as `lead_context` and read\nlater by an email agent writing first-touch outreach.\n\n## What you are given\n\n- **The lead**: name, job title, the company they work for, and its industry.\n- **The published post**: a summary of an article our side just published.\n\n## What to do\n\n1. Search the web for what is currently true about **this company** and **this\n   industry**, focused on the themes in the published post.\n2. Write a note that connects the post's themes to that specific company's\n   situation — what they actually do, how the theme lands for them, and the one\n   question a salesperson could open with.\n\n## Rules\n\n- **Ground every claim.** If the search did not support it, do not write it.\n  No invented funding rounds, headcounts, customers, or product names.\n- **Be specific to this company.** A note that would read identically for any\n  company in the industry is a failed note.\n- **Never address the lead directly** and never write email copy. This is\n  context *for* the writer, not the message itself.\n- Target ~160 words, one paragraph, plain prose — no headings, no\n  bullet lists, no markdown.\n- End with one open question a rep could ask, phrased for a first conversation.\n- If the search finds little about the company, say so plainly and stay at the\n  industry level rather than padding with generic claims.\n"}}
{"stream": "process", "run_id": "res-6f2cda551cbf", "event": "model_response", "ts": 1787836743.4451773, "model": "claude-sonnet-4-6", "duration_ms": 18879.1, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "input_tokens": 19009, "output_tokens": 653, "web_search_requests": 2, "searches": 2, "sources": 18, "source_urls": ["https://fintech.global/2026/02/05/stash-secured-one-of-the-top-us-wealthtech-deals-as-funding-in-the-country-fell-by-56-in-2025-2/", "https://investors.grab.com/news-and-events/news-details/2026/Grab-Accelerates-Financial-Services-Roadmap-with-Acquisition-of-Digital-Investing-Platform-Stash-Financial-Inc--2026-5wydDSQuVA/default.aspx", "https://www.stash.com/press", "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1855612/000185561226000009/projectsapphire_pressrelea.htm", "https://www.bankingdive.com/news/fintech-stash-gets-a-boost-from-its-ai-assistant-money-coach/740418/", "https://www.bankingdive.com/news/grab-singapore-app-acquire-stash-fintech-ai-investing-banking/812490/", "https://www.stash.com/news//stash-secures-146m-series-h/", "https://tracxn.com/d/companies/stash/__-6gpJpjrhpRjjMLHcCBB6dyYEedk07H9OQMtJdQawKs", "https://www.prnewswire.com/news-releases/with-4-3b-under-management-stash-secures-146m-series-h-to-lead-the-next-era-of-ai-driven-financial-guidance-302451796.html", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stash_(company)", "https://www.venable.com/insights/publications/2026/02/ai-in-financial-services-popular-use-cases", "https://nationalmortgageprofessional.com/news/cfpb-issues-ai-underwriting-guidance-adverse-action-notices", "https://www.lexology.com/library/detail.aspx?g=536c2179-f411-4793-ae9c-1bc3c44e3651", "https://elevateconsult.com/insights/proven-ways-to-avoid-1m-model-risk-penaltiesai-governance-in-fintech-avoid-1m-risk-penalties/", "https://www.goodwinlaw.com/en/insights/publications/2026/01/insights-cldr-practices-key-trends-of-2025-state-legislation-impacting-cfs", "https://www.consumerfinance.gov/archive/blog/innovation-spotlight-providing-adverse-action-notices-when-using-ai-ml-models/", "https://www.shumaker.com/insight/client-alert-generative-artificial-intelligence-in-financial-services-a-practical-compliance-playbook-for-2026/", "https://hesfintech.com/blog/all-legislative-trends-regulating-ai-in-lending/"], "chars": 1930, "text_preview": "Here is the `lead_context` note for Tejeshwar Thodeti:\n\n---\n\nStash is an AI-powered investing app with $5 billion in AUM and over a million paying subscribers, and it is now navigating a significant transition: Grab Holdings has signed definitive agreements to acquire 100% of Stash Financial, with the transaction expected to close in Q3 2026 pending regulatory approvals. Central to the company's current product strategy is its AI Money Coach, which is built for regulated financial services and combines expert knowledge, deep financial integrations, and compliance-by-design — with interactions described as auditable and configurable through defined policies and controls. Despite this stated posture, the broader regulatory environment is tightening rapidly: the CFPB issued Circular 2026-03 in May 2026, advising that lenders using ML models remain fully responsible under ECOA and Regulation B for providing specific, accurate reasons for adverse action, while FINRA's 2026 Annual Regulatory Oversight Report now dedicates an entire section to the fact that generative AI is no longer a novelty — it is a supervised technology demanding the same compliance rigor as any critical system. Colorado's AI Act, effective June 30, 2026, imposes requirements on developers of high-risk AI systems that include those with a material effect on the provision of financial services. As Head of Operations, Thodeti sits at the intersection of AI deployment and operational accountability — the exact layer where governance gaps (decision logging, model version control, human review gates, and audit-readiness) tend to surface first under examiner scrutiny. A strong opening question for a first conversation: \"As Money Coach scales and the Grab acquisition brings new regulatory touch points, how are you currently thinking about building the audit trail and oversight layer around AI-driven guidance before regulators come asking?\""}
