{"stream": "process", "run_id": "res-6f2cda551cbf", "event": "model_request", "ts": 1787836748.7586133, "model": "claude-sonnet-4-6", "max_tokens": 2000, "search_enabled": true, "search_tool": "web_search_20250305", "search_max_uses": 5, "timeout_s": 90, "prompt_chars": 2991, "system_chars": 1496, "endpoint": "anthropic.messages.create", "sent_keys": ["max_tokens", "messages", "model", "system", "tools"], "model_configured": "", "allowed_domains": [], "blocked_domains": [], "system_preview": "# Research prompt — lead_context\n\nYou are the Research Agent for a B2B lead-qualification system. You produce ONE\nshort note that will be stored on a HubSpot contact as `lead_context` and read\nlater by an email agent writing first-touch outreach.\n\n## What you are given\n\n- **The lead**: name, job title, the company they work for, and its industry.\n- **The published post**: a summary of an article our side just published.\n\n## What to do\n\n1. Search the web for what is currently true about **this company** and **this\n   industry**, focused on the themes in the published post.\n2. Write a note that connects the post's themes to that specific company's\n   situation — what they actually do, how the theme lands for them, and the one\n   question a salesperson could open with.\n\n## Rules\n\n- **Ground every claim.** If the search did not support it, do not write it.\n  No invented funding rounds, headcounts, customers, or product names.\n- **Be specific to this company.** A note that would read identically for any\n  company in the industry is a failed note.\n- **Never address the lead directly** and never write email copy. This is\n  context *for* the writer, not the message itself.\n- Target ~160 words, one paragraph, plain prose — no headings, no\n  bullet lists, no markdown.\n- End with one open question a rep could ask, phrased for a first conversation.\n- If the search finds little about the company, say so plainly and stay at the\n  industry level rather than padding with generic claims.\n", "prompt_preview": "Research this lead and write the lead_context note.\n\n## The lead\n- Name: Mahitha Naganaboina\n- Job title: Vice President\n- Company: M1 Finance\n- Industry: FINANCIAL_SERVICES\n- Company website: https://m1.com\n- About the company: Fintech platform offering commission-free automated investing, high-yield cash accounts, and margin loans, letting users build customizable brokerage, retirement, and crypto portfolios.\n\n## The post we published\n- Industry: FINANCIAL_SERVICES\n- Published: 2026-08-24T15:00:00Z\n- Summary:\nAI in Claims and Underwriting: The Governance to Show\n\nThis document argues that in insurance, AI-generated outputs in claims and underwriting are effectively regulated decisions, so the control layer (citations, human review, logging) must be a core part of the product rather than an afterthought. It describes regulatory expectations, including the NAIC's model bulletin, FCRA adverse action rules, and state unfair claims settlement practices laws, which require carriers to justify denials with specific policy language. It outlines what market conduct examiners look for and recommends building an 'exam-ready' evidence pack in advance, including an AI inventory, written governance program, decision logs, testing evidence, and complaint linkage. The document also stresses that models, prompts, and retrieval corpora change over time, so carriers need version pinning, frozen evaluation sets, override tracking, and formal change approval to maintain defensibility. It closes by referencing AI Definitive's 'Governed-by-Design' approach and a free Claude Readiness Assessment.\n\nKey points:\n- AI drafts in claims/underwriting can become regulated decisions instantly, so governance must be built into the product itself.\n- Retrieval must cite the exact policy form, edition, and endorsement in force for the specific policy and date of loss.\n- Denials require a second human gate: a licensed adjuster or underwriter reviews and approves before the decision is finalized.\n- Regulatory frameworks include the NAIC model bulletin, FCRA adverse action rules, and state unfair claims settlement practices laws, with expectations varying by state.\n- Carriers with multi-state footprints should build to the strictest state standard rather than running multiple explainability standards.\n- Market conduct examiners expect an AI inventory, written governance program, decision logs, testing evidence, and complaint traceability, ideally prepared in advance.\n- Model, prompt, and retrieval corpus changes require version pinning, frozen evaluation sets re-run before promotion, override tracking, and formal change approval.\n- A rising adjuster override rate is an early signal of model drift, often before other metrics show it.\n\nTechnologies: Claude, NAIC model bulletin on AI use, FCRA (Fair Credit Reporting Act)\n\n## Your task\nSearch the web for what is true right now about M1 Finance and the FINANCIAL_SERVICES industry, focused on the themes above. Then write the note.", "payload": {"model": "claude-sonnet-4-6", "max_tokens": 2000, "messages": [{"role": "user", "content": "Research this lead and write the lead_context note.\n\n## The lead\n- Name: Mahitha Naganaboina\n- Job title: Vice President\n- Company: M1 Finance\n- Industry: FINANCIAL_SERVICES\n- Company website: https://m1.com\n- About the company: Fintech platform offering commission-free automated investing, high-yield cash accounts, and margin loans, letting users build customizable brokerage, retirement, and crypto portfolios.\n\n## The post we published\n- Industry: FINANCIAL_SERVICES\n- Published: 2026-08-24T15:00:00Z\n- Summary:\nAI in Claims and Underwriting: The Governance to Show\n\nThis document argues that in insurance, AI-generated outputs in claims and underwriting are effectively regulated decisions, so the control layer (citations, human review, logging) must be a core part of the product rather than an afterthought. It describes regulatory expectations, including the NAIC's model bulletin, FCRA adverse action rules, and state unfair claims settlement practices laws, which require carriers to justify denials with specific policy language. It outlines what market conduct examiners look for and recommends building an 'exam-ready' evidence pack in advance, including an AI inventory, written governance program, decision logs, testing evidence, and complaint linkage. The document also stresses that models, prompts, and retrieval corpora change over time, so carriers need version pinning, frozen evaluation sets, override tracking, and formal change approval to maintain defensibility. It closes by referencing AI Definitive's 'Governed-by-Design' approach and a free Claude Readiness Assessment.\n\nKey points:\n- AI drafts in claims/underwriting can become regulated decisions instantly, so governance must be built into the product itself.\n- Retrieval must cite the exact policy form, edition, and endorsement in force for the specific policy and date of loss.\n- Denials require a second human gate: a licensed adjuster or underwriter reviews and approves before the decision is finalized.\n- Regulatory frameworks include the NAIC model bulletin, FCRA adverse action rules, and state unfair claims settlement practices laws, with expectations varying by state.\n- Carriers with multi-state footprints should build to the strictest state standard rather than running multiple explainability standards.\n- Market conduct examiners expect an AI inventory, written governance program, decision logs, testing evidence, and complaint traceability, ideally prepared in advance.\n- Model, prompt, and retrieval corpus changes require version pinning, frozen evaluation sets re-run before promotion, override tracking, and formal change approval.\n- A rising adjuster override rate is an early signal of model drift, often before other metrics show it.\n\nTechnologies: Claude, NAIC model bulletin on AI use, FCRA (Fair Credit Reporting Act)\n\n## Your task\nSearch the web for what is true right now about M1 Finance and the FINANCIAL_SERVICES industry, focused on the themes above. Then write the note."}], "tools": [{"type": "web_search_20250305", "name": "web_search", "max_uses": 5}], "system": "# Research prompt — lead_context\n\nYou are the Research Agent for a B2B lead-qualification system. You produce ONE\nshort note that will be stored on a HubSpot contact as `lead_context` and read\nlater by an email agent writing first-touch outreach.\n\n## What you are given\n\n- **The lead**: name, job title, the company they work for, and its industry.\n- **The published post**: a summary of an article our side just published.\n\n## What to do\n\n1. Search the web for what is currently true about **this company** and **this\n   industry**, focused on the themes in the published post.\n2. Write a note that connects the post's themes to that specific company's\n   situation — what they actually do, how the theme lands for them, and the one\n   question a salesperson could open with.\n\n## Rules\n\n- **Ground every claim.** If the search did not support it, do not write it.\n  No invented funding rounds, headcounts, customers, or product names.\n- **Be specific to this company.** A note that would read identically for any\n  company in the industry is a failed note.\n- **Never address the lead directly** and never write email copy. This is\n  context *for* the writer, not the message itself.\n- Target ~160 words, one paragraph, plain prose — no headings, no\n  bullet lists, no markdown.\n- End with one open question a rep could ask, phrased for a first conversation.\n- If the search finds little about the company, say so plainly and stay at the\n  industry level rather than padding with generic claims.\n"}}
{"stream": "process", "run_id": "res-6f2cda551cbf", "event": "model_response", "ts": 1787836772.0344546, "model": "claude-sonnet-4-6", "duration_ms": 23272.2, "stop_reason": "end_turn", "input_tokens": 38492, "output_tokens": 693, "web_search_requests": 3, "searches": 3, "sources": 26, "source_urls": ["https://builtin.com/company/m1/faq/stability-growth", "https://www.nerdwallet.com/investing/reviews/m1-finance", "https://m1.com/about/newsroom/", "https://m1.com/blog/category/invest/", "https://m1.com/blog/our-year-together/", "https://m1.com/blog/introducing-news-snapshot/", "https://tradersunion.com/news/tag/m1-finance/", "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/M1_Finance", "https://m1.com/blog/", "https://fintech.global/2026/01/08/ai-regulatory-compliance-priorities-financial-institutions-face-in-2026/", "https://www.citrincooperman.com/In-Focus-Resource-Center/Regulatory-Changes-Affecting-Financial-Services-in-2026", "https://www.innreg.com/blog/fintech-regulation-guide-for-startups", "https://fintech.global/2026/08/17/ai-governance-gap-puts-financial-firms-at-examination-risk/", "https://www.bakermckenzie.com/en/insight/publications/2026/01/financial-institutions-in-a-digital-and-regulatory-reset", "https://www.wolterskluwer.com/en/expert-insights/the-fintech-landscape-in-2026", "https://fintech.global/2026/08/07/sec-ai-checks-put-firms-governance-under-scrutiny/", "https://wezom.com/blog/fintech-development-trends-2026", "https://www.finra.org/media-center/newsreleases/2024/finra-fines-m1-finance-850000-violations-regarding-use-social-media", "https://reports.adviserinfo.sec.gov/crs/crs_249787.pdf", "https://files.brokercheck.finra.org/crs_281242.pdf", "https://www.americanbar.org/groups/business_law/resources/business-law-today/2023-november/adverse-action-notice-compliance-considerations-for-creditors-that-use-ai/", "https://www.sec.gov/Archives/edgar/data/1650711/000165071126000002/2025.M1.audit.PUBLIC.O.pdf", "https://m1.com/borrow/margin-loans/invest-with-margin/", "https://aurascape.ai/answers/ai-compliance-frameworks-standards-and-governance-for-banks-investment-firms/", "https://www.innreg.com/blog/ai-in-financial-services", "https://www.sidley.com/en/insights/newsupdates/2025/02/artificial-intelligence-us-financial-regulator-guidelines-for-responsible-use"], "chars": 1829, "text_preview": "Here is the `lead_context` note for Mahitha Naganaboina at M1 Finance:\n\n---\n\nM1 is a technology company offering a range of financial products and services, and it is actively differentiating through its customizable \"Pies\" system and feature rollouts that include an AI assistant beta. That AI buildout carries real regulatory weight. M1 Finance LLC is registered with the SEC as a broker-dealer and is a member of FINRA and SIPC, and it makes revenue from margin loans — meaning any AI-assisted margin eligibility or account-action decision immediately enters FCRA and ECOA adverse-action territory. Where a decision is made by AI, a lack of clarity about the model's design may heighten regulatory concerns, and creditors should design and employ AI models that are explainable enough to satisfy adverse-action notice obligations under both the ECOA and the FCRA. The examination pressure is real and current: FINRA's 2026 Annual Regulatory Oversight Report added a dedicated section on generative AI, asking member firms to show evidence of testing, supervision, governance, vendor diligence, and recordkeeping — and examiners will expect this regardless of whether a firm actively markets AI-powered strategies. M1 already has a prior FINRA enforcement action tied to margin-lending communications, so regulators have a lens on that product line specifically. The post's argument — that governance, logging, and human-review gates must be built into AI features rather than bolted on — maps precisely to M1's moment of shipping an AI assistant into a broker-dealer environment. Opening question for the rep: *When the AI assistant beta interacts with margin-loan decisions or account restrictions, what does M1's current governance layer look like for logging those outputs and producing an audit trail if an examiner asks?*"}
